 ```
 
+### Parallel execution with communication
+
+Set `communication: true` on a parallel `subagent` call. Each task may set an `id`; otherwise IDs are generated as `<agent>-<occurrence>`, such as `scout-1` and `scout-2`.
+
+```json
+{
+  "tasks": [
+    { "id": "api-scout", "agent": "scout", "task": "Find the provider interface constraints" },
+    { "id": "implementer", "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement the provider change" },
+    { "id": "reviewer", "agent": "reviewer", "task": "Review changes as they appear" }
+  ],
+  "communication": true
+}
+```
+
+Communication adds these tools to each worker:
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result |
+|------|-----------|--------|
+| `subagent_teammates` | `{}` | Reports the caller's worker ID and every teammate's agent, task, and status |
+| `subagent_send` | `{ recipient, message }` | Accepts a private message for one running teammate |
+| `subagent_broadcast` | `{ message }` | Accepts a message for all other running teammates and reports per-recipient failures |
+
+Example worker calls:
+
+```json
+{ "recipient": "implementer", "message": "Provider.create() requires an explicit timeout; the default path is not safe." }
+```
+
+```json
+{ "message": "The old cache adapter cannot support atomic compare-and-set; avoid that approach." }
+```
+
+Messages are injected automatically into the recipient's next model call after any currently running tools finish. The recipient does not poll an inbox, and acceptance does not wait for a reply. Messages are limited to 64 KiB of UTF-8 text. A dispatch uses a fresh authenticated loopback server; it is closed on completion or cancellation, so identities and messages are not reused by later dispatches.
+
+**Delivery design:** A print-mode worker could exit between finishing its answer and receiving a late finding. Communication-enabled workers therefore remain separate Pi processes but run through Pi's RPC mode. The parent serializes delivery acceptance with worker shutdown, queues accepted messages with `streamingBehavior: "steer"`, and closes each worker only after Pi reports it idle. For example, a finding accepted while the recipient runs a shell tool waits for that tool, enters the next model call, and is processed before the worker can exit.
+
